course: dictionary
lesson: 8589
title: Scold and scolding
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To scold someone is to speak to them angrily because they have done something wrong. «The priest did not scold him for coming back.» Here the priest did not tell him off, so he was not in trouble for coming back.
explain: Scold is a verb, so it changes its ending: scolds, scolded, scolding. A scolding is the angry telling-off itself. Ella got a good scolding for forgetting her homework means she was told off angrily, and a good scolding is a strong one.
explain: Context helps here too. When you see the words in trouble, wrong or angrily nearby, scold is probably about being told off. You can check by swapping in "tell off" and seeing if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: «Scold» means to speak angrily to someone because they have done something wrong.
fact: «A scolding» is an angry telling-off.
fact: In the UK people often say «tell off», and in Australia they say «growl at» instead of scold.
fact: Old-fashioned: a scold was a person, especially a woman, who often nagged or complained.
fact: In Mandarin, «mà» (said with a sharp falling voice) means to scold, which is a different word from «mā» (mother).
exercise: «The priest did not scold him for coming back.» What does scold mean? | To hug someone warmly | To run away quickly | To give someone a present | * To speak angrily to someone for doing something wrong
exercise: Ella got a good scolding for forgetting her homework. What is a scolding? | A type of cake | A kind hug | A long holiday | * An angry telling-off
exercise: In Australia people often say «growl at» instead of scold. What does growl at mean here? | To sing a happy song | To wave goodbye | * To scold someone | To cheer someone on
test: Yesterday the teacher ___ Sam for talking in class. | scolds | * scolded | scold | scolding
test: «Alice snaps "Nobody asked your opinion", and the Hatter asks "Who's making personal remarks now?" She has done the very thing she scolded him for.» What did Alice do? | * She did the very thing she scolded the Hatter for | She gave the Hatter a present | She said sorry for everything | She fell asleep
exam: Which phrase means a telling-off? | a long nap | a loud song | a warm hug | * a good scolding
